    "intro": "Good lake names say what kind of water it is, and the word for lake does half the work: a <em>loch</em> sounds Scottish, a <em>tarn</em> high and cold, a <em>mere</em> quiet and English. This **lake name generator** gives you fantasy lakes for your D&amp;D map, British-style lochs, meres, tarns and llyns, dark and haunted waters and crystal-clear magical lakes, with a short description if you want a story to go with one.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Eight good lake names",
        "content": "<p>All eight came from the generator, across its styles:</p><ul><li><strong>The Lake of the Seven Isles</strong>: a fantasy lake with a map in its name.</li><li><strong>Jorvane Lake, that fills a crater left by a falling star</strong>: a lake with an origin story.</li><li><strong>Llyn y Frân</strong>: Welsh for \"lake of the crow\".</li><li><strong>Loch Tarvach</strong>: a Scottish-sounding loch.</li><li><strong>Snipe Tarn</strong>: a small mountain lake named after a bird that nests there.</li><li><strong>Stag Tarn, where a crannog once stood on a little island</strong>: a British lake with real history in its description.</li><li><strong>Hangman's Mere</strong>: a dark water nobody swims in.</li><li><strong>Moonbow Lake</strong>: a crystal-clear magical lake.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Meres, lochs, llyns and tarns",
        "content": "<p>Each part of Britain has its own word for a lake:</p><ul><li><strong>Mere</strong> is Old English, used for lakes in England, and is part of Windermere, which means the lake of a Norseman called Vinandr.</li><li><strong>Loch</strong> is the Gaelic word, used across Scotland.</li><li><strong>Llyn</strong> is Welsh. Welsh lake names often describe something: <em>Llyn y Wrach</em> is the witch's lake, <em>Llyn Dwfn</em> the deep lake.</li><li><strong>Tarn</strong>, from the Old Norse <em>tjörn</em>, is a small mountain lake, common in the Lake District.</li></ul><p>Oddly, only one of the Lake District's lakes, Bassenthwaite Lake, has \"lake\" in its name. The rest are meres, waters and tarns.</p><p>Choose <strong>British</strong> for names like these, <strong>Fantasy</strong> for <em>Lake Lorrendal</em>, <strong>Dark</strong> for <em>Drowned Maid's Pool</em>, or <strong>Crystal</strong> for <em>the Starlit Pool</em>.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Lakes in your world",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Give a lake a reason to matter.</strong> A crossing, a border, an island fortress or something underneath the water.</li><li><strong>Match the word to the region.</strong> If the hills are fells, the lakes are probably meres and tarns; if they're bens, use lochs.</li><li><strong>Use the description as a rumour</strong> for an innkeeper to tell.</li></ul><p>For the rivers that feed it, try the <a href=\"river-name-generator.html\">River Name Generator</a>, and for the hills around it, the <a href=\"mountain-name-generator.html\">Mountain</a> and <a href=\"forest-name-generator.html\">Forest</a> name generators. For a much bigger body of water, there's the <a href=\"sea-name-generator.html\">Sea Name Generator</a>, and for the land it sits in, the <a href=\"continent-name-generator.html\">Continent</a> and <a href=\"world-name-generator.html\">World</a> name generators.</p><p>For more, see all the <a href=\"../categories/worldbuilding-names.html\">world-building name generators</a>.</p>"
